# Line endings, first.
#
# Without this, a checkout on Windows gets CRLF and every file written by the
# scripts inherits it -- so a skill packaged there ships different bytes from
# the same skill packaged on Linux, and diffs turn into whole-file rewrites.
# `text=auto eol=lf` stores and checks out LF everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so git never tries to normalise them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.zip binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What a download contains
#
# Two audiences, and they don't arrive the same way:
#
#   Using it     -> the release page. Wants the skill and enough prose to
#                   install it. Has no use for a test suite.
#   Changing it  -> `git clone`. Wants everything, and gets everything:
#                   `export-ignore` has no effect on a clone.
#
# So the lines below trim development scaffolding out of anything built with
# `git archive` -- both "Source code" downloads on a release, and the green
# Code > Download ZIP button. Cloning is unaffected, which is why CONTRIBUTING
# tells contributors to clone rather than download.
#
# Two things this cannot do, written here so they aren't rediscovered:
#
#   1. It cannot remove the "Source code" downloads. GitHub attaches them to
#      every release and there is no setting to turn them off. It can only
#      make them smaller.
#   2. It cannot produce the archive claude.ai accepts. That has to be rooted
#      at the skill folder, and `git archive` always roots at `<repo>-<tag>/`,
#      leaving the skill under `skills/`. The upload is
#      `skill-boilerplate.zip`, built by `scripts/build_skill_zip.py` and
#      attached by `.github/workflows/release.yml`.
#
# Kept in: `skills/` (the skill itself), `template/` (the no-wizard path),
# `docs/`, `README.md`, `LICENSE`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `.claude-plugin/`.
/tests          export-ignore
/scripts        export-ignore
/.github        export-ignore
/.gitattributes export-ignore
/.gitignore     export-ignore
